1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,334
Three foods hide something no one could

2
00:00:02,334 --> 00:00:04,669
see without an instrument. Here is the

3
00:00:04,669 --> 00:00:07,003
whole video, one food at a time.

4
00:00:07,553 --> 00:00:11,230
Chili. Chili peppers taste hot because capsaicin binds

5
00:00:11,230 --> 00:00:14,906
to TRPV1, the same receptor on pain-sensing nerves

6
00:00:14,906 --> 00:00:18,583
that normally fires when tissue is heated above

7
00:00:18,583 --> 00:00:20,881
about 43 degrees Celsius. Coffee.

8
00:00:21,041 --> 00:00:24,517
Coffee tastes bitter mainly because roasting breaks the

9
00:00:24,517 --> 00:00:27,993
bean's chlorogenic acids into new bitter molecules, which

10
00:00:27,993 --> 00:00:31,469
bind bitter taste receptors on the tongue; caffeine

11
00:00:31,469 --> 00:00:34,945
contributes, but only part of the effect. Honey.

12
00:00:35,105 --> 00:00:37,685
Honey resists spoiling because it holds so

13
00:00:37,685 --> 00:00:40,264
little water, and so much sugar, that

14
00:00:40,264 --> 00:00:42,844
microbes cannot draw enough water to grow.

15
00:00:43,394 --> 00:00:45,657
None of this was found by tasting. It

16
00:00:45,657 --> 00:00:47,920
was found by measuring - by pulling a

17
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:50,183
food apart until its parts could be counted.

18
00:00:50,343 --> 00:00:53,063
By the end you will know what

19
00:00:53,063 --> 00:00:55,783
is inside chili, coffee and honey, and

20
00:00:55,783 --> 00:00:58,114
how anyone found out. Chili first.

21
00:00:58,664 --> 00:01:01,933
Chili, Coffee, Honey. What's Inside?

22
00:01:02,467 --> 00:01:04,360
Why do chili peppers taste hot?

23
00:01:04,910 --> 00:01:08,026
A thermometer on your tongue reads normal while

24
00:01:08,026 --> 00:01:11,142
the burn builds. Chili peppers taste hot because

25
00:01:11,142 --> 00:01:14,257
capsaicin binds to TRPV1, the same receptor on

26
00:01:14,257 --> 00:01:17,373
pain-sensing nerves that normally fires when tissue is

27
00:01:17,373 --> 00:01:19,710
heated above about 43 degrees Celsius.

28
00:01:19,870 --> 00:01:22,625
The chili never warms your mouth; the nerve

29
00:01:22,625 --> 00:01:25,380
only reports that it does. That threshold comes

30
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:28,134
from the work that identified the receptor, published

31
00:01:28,134 --> 00:01:30,889
in the journal Nature in 1997. It settles

32
00:01:30,889 --> 00:01:32,611
less than it seems to.

33
00:01:32,771 --> 00:01:35,795
It explains why the burn feels like heat.

34
00:01:35,955 --> 00:01:38,318
It does not explain why a plant

35
00:01:38,318 --> 00:01:40,681
would make such a molecule, why some

36
00:01:40,681 --> 00:01:43,043
animals eat chilies without flinching, or how

37
00:01:43,043 --> 00:01:45,406
anyone put a number on a sensation

38
00:01:45,406 --> 00:01:47,769
that exists only inside a nervous system.

39
00:01:48,319 --> 00:01:51,247
Start with where the burn is not. Taste

40
00:01:51,247 --> 00:01:54,176
buds carry receptors for a small set of

41
00:01:54,176 --> 00:01:57,104
tastes: sweet, sour, salty, bitter and savoury. Heat

42
00:01:57,104 --> 00:02:00,033
is not on that list, and the evidence

43
00:02:00,033 --> 00:02:01,863
for that needs no instrument.

44
00:02:02,023 --> 00:02:04,501
Chili juice on the skin of a hand,

45
00:02:04,501 --> 00:02:06,979
or near an eye, produces the same burn

46
00:02:06,979 --> 00:02:09,458
in tissue that has no taste buds at

47
00:02:09,458 --> 00:02:11,936
all. Whatever capsaicin is doing, it is not

48
00:02:11,936 --> 00:02:13,175
doing it through taste.

49
00:02:13,335 --> 00:02:15,573
That simple observation was the first

50
00:02:15,573 --> 00:02:17,812
clue, and it pointed researchers away

51
00:02:17,812 --> 00:02:20,050
from the tongue's taste machinery entirely.

52
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:23,452
So the sensation travels by a different route.

53
00:02:23,452 --> 00:02:26,304
In the mouth, the burn is carried largely

54
00:02:26,304 --> 00:02:29,156
by the trigeminal nerve, the same nerve that

55
00:02:29,156 --> 00:02:32,008
reports touch, temperature and pain across the face.

56
00:02:32,168 --> 00:02:35,199
Researchers call this family of sensations chemesthesis:

57
00:02:35,199 --> 00:02:38,229
chemicals triggering senses that normally respond to

58
00:02:38,229 --> 00:02:41,260
physical events. The sting of mustard and

59
00:02:41,260 --> 00:02:44,291
the cool of mint belong here too.

60
00:02:44,451 --> 00:02:46,911
Chili heat is chemesthesis, not taste, and

61
00:02:46,911 --> 00:02:49,371
that distinction told researchers where to look.

62
00:02:49,371 --> 00:02:51,831
The answer would be found on a

63
00:02:51,831 --> 00:02:54,291
nerve ending, not inside a taste bud.

64
00:02:54,841 --> 00:02:57,699
For decades, physiologists knew capsaicin excited a

65
00:02:57,699 --> 00:03:00,558
particular class of pain-sensing neurons, but nobody

66
00:03:00,558 --> 00:03:03,416
knew what it actually bound to. The

67
00:03:03,416 --> 00:03:06,274
laboratory of David Julius approached the problem

68
00:03:06,274 --> 00:03:08,724
by fishing with the molecule itself.

69
00:03:08,884 --> 00:03:11,609
The team built a library of genes active

70
00:03:11,609 --> 00:03:14,334
in sensory neurons, then put pools of those

71
00:03:14,334 --> 00:03:16,718
genes into cells that normally ignore capsaicin.

72
00:03:16,878 --> 00:03:19,613
They added capsaicin and watched for calcium flooding

73
00:03:19,613 --> 00:03:22,348
into the cells, made visible by a dye

74
00:03:22,348 --> 00:03:25,083
that glows when calcium rises. A pool that

75
00:03:25,083 --> 00:03:27,476
lit up contained the gene they wanted.

76
00:03:27,636 --> 00:03:30,225
Split the pool, test again, and

77
00:03:30,225 --> 00:03:32,814
keep going until one gene remains.

78
00:03:33,364 --> 00:03:36,345
The gene they isolated encoded an ion channel,

79
00:03:36,345 --> 00:03:39,325
a pore in the nerve cell's membrane that

80
00:03:39,325 --> 00:03:41,561
opens to let charged particles through.

81
00:03:41,721 --> 00:03:44,425
They called it the vanilloid receptor, after

82
00:03:44,425 --> 00:03:47,129
a chemical group capsaicin shares with vanillin,

83
00:03:47,129 --> 00:03:49,833
the main flavour molecule of vanilla. Today

84
00:03:49,833 --> 00:03:51,764
it is known as TRPV1.

85
00:03:51,924 --> 00:03:54,561
The paper, published in Nature in 1997,

86
00:03:54,561 --> 00:03:57,198
reported something that turned the whole question

87
00:03:57,198 --> 00:03:59,834
around. The channel did not only open

88
00:03:59,834 --> 00:04:02,471
for capsaicin. It also opened for heat.

89
00:04:03,021 --> 00:04:05,754
In the same experiments, cells carrying TRPV1

90
00:04:05,754 --> 00:04:08,487
responded when the surrounding solution was warmed

91
00:04:08,487 --> 00:04:11,220
above about 43 degrees Celsius, a temperature

92
00:04:11,220 --> 00:04:13,953
at which contact begins to register as

93
00:04:13,953 --> 00:04:16,296
painful. That is the load-bearing fact.

94
00:04:16,456 --> 00:04:19,019
A nerve ending has one way to

95
00:04:19,019 --> 00:04:21,582
signal: it fires or it stays quiet.

96
00:04:21,582 --> 00:04:24,146
When capsaicin binds TRPV1, the channel opens,

97
00:04:24,146 --> 00:04:26,709
the nerve fires, and the brain receives

98
00:04:26,709 --> 00:04:29,272
the same message a scald would send.

99
00:04:29,432 --> 00:04:32,230
It has no separate line that says this

100
00:04:32,230 --> 00:04:35,029
came from a molecule. So the brain reports

101
00:04:35,029 --> 00:04:37,828
heat. Nothing in a chili warms your mouth,

102
00:04:37,828 --> 00:04:40,626
and no thermometer would ever show the burn.

103
00:04:41,176 --> 00:04:44,479
Later work filled in the receptor's other sensitivities.

104
00:04:44,479 --> 00:04:47,782
Alongside capsaicin and heat, TRPV1 also responds to

105
00:04:47,782 --> 00:04:51,084
acidity. It does not respond to cold; the

106
00:04:51,084 --> 00:04:54,387
cooling of menthol works through a different channel.

107
00:04:54,547 --> 00:04:56,996
And the receptor sits mostly on pain-sensing

108
00:04:56,996 --> 00:04:59,446
nerves, which is why the chili sensation

109
00:04:59,446 --> 00:05:01,895
arrives as a burn rather than a

110
00:05:01,895 --> 00:05:04,344
pleasant warmth. Capsaicin also explains the lingering.

111
00:05:04,504 --> 00:05:06,624
It is an oily molecule that dissolves

112
00:05:06,624 --> 00:05:08,744
poorly in water, so it clings to

113
00:05:08,744 --> 00:05:10,864
tissue and keeps the channel busy after

114
00:05:10,864 --> 00:05:12,075
the food is gone.

115
00:05:12,625 --> 00:05:15,486
Identifying a receptor is not the same as

116
00:05:15,486 --> 00:05:18,347
proving it is the one that matters. The

117
00:05:18,347 --> 00:05:21,207
decisive test came from mice, in work published

118
00:05:21,207 --> 00:05:23,353
in the journal Science in 2000.

119
00:05:23,513 --> 00:05:26,415
Researchers bred animals that lacked the TRPV1 gene

120
00:05:26,415 --> 00:05:29,318
and offered them capsaicin water, ordinary drinking water

121
00:05:29,318 --> 00:05:32,220
laced with the molecule. Normal mice avoid it.

122
00:05:32,220 --> 00:05:35,123
The mice without the receptor drank it as

123
00:05:35,123 --> 00:05:36,937
if it were plain water.

124
00:05:37,097 --> 00:05:39,974
Their response to high heat was also

125
00:05:39,974 --> 00:05:42,850
dulled, though not erased, which hinted that

126
00:05:42,850 --> 00:05:45,316
other heat sensors work alongside TRPV1.

127
00:05:45,476 --> 00:05:48,117
That combination, a molecule that opens a channel

128
00:05:48,117 --> 00:05:50,758
in a dish and an animal that stops

129
00:05:50,758 --> 00:05:53,399
caring once the channel is gone, is what

130
00:05:53,399 --> 00:05:55,380
turns a candidate into a mechanism.

131
00:05:55,930 --> 00:05:58,237
A pepper plant fits that description. It

132
00:05:58,237 --> 00:06:00,544
is not a passive bowl of flavour;

133
00:06:00,544 --> 00:06:02,851
it is running chemistry. Capsaicin is made

134
00:06:02,851 --> 00:06:05,158
in the placenta, the pale tissue inside

135
00:06:05,158 --> 00:06:07,135
the fruit that holds the seeds.

136
00:06:07,295 --> 00:06:09,698
The seeds themselves make little of it, but

137
00:06:09,698 --> 00:06:12,101
they sit pressed against the placenta and pick

138
00:06:12,101 --> 00:06:14,504
it up, which is why the pith carries

139
00:06:14,504 --> 00:06:16,306
more heat than the outer flesh.

140
00:06:16,466 --> 00:06:20,233
Capsaicin is the best known of a small

141
00:06:20,233 --> 00:06:24,000
family of related compounds called capsaicinoids. PubChem, the

142
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,766
public chemical database maintained by the National Institutes

143
00:06:27,766 --> 00:06:31,533
of Health, records its makeup: eighteen carbon atoms,

144
00:06:31,533 --> 00:06:34,829
twenty-seven hydrogens, one nitrogen and three oxygens.

145
00:06:35,379 --> 00:06:38,185
Bell peppers belong to the same species as

146
00:06:38,185 --> 00:06:40,992
many hot chilies, yet a bell pepper registers

147
00:06:40,992 --> 00:06:43,798
zero heat. It is not a mild chili.

148
00:06:43,798 --> 00:06:46,254
It does not make capsaicin at all.

149
00:06:46,414 --> 00:06:48,617
That single difference, a working pathway or

150
00:06:48,617 --> 00:06:50,819
a broken one, shows that heat is

151
00:06:50,819 --> 00:06:53,022
a property of the plant's chemistry, not

152
00:06:53,022 --> 00:06:55,225
of the species name, the shape of

153
00:06:55,225 --> 00:06:56,798
the fruit or its colour.

154
00:06:56,958 --> 00:06:59,574
Flavour and heat are separate traits, and a

155
00:06:59,574 --> 00:07:02,190
plant can keep one while losing the other.

156
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:06,010
The obvious next question is why a plant

157
00:07:06,010 --> 00:07:09,280
would spend resources making this molecule. The leading

158
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:12,550
explanation is called directed deterrence, and it comes

159
00:07:12,550 --> 00:07:15,820
from feeding studies, including one published in Nature,

160
00:07:15,820 --> 00:07:18,681
that watched which animals ate wild chilies.

161
00:07:18,841 --> 00:07:21,614
Rodents avoided the fruit. Birds ate it

162
00:07:21,614 --> 00:07:24,387
freely. That split matters because the two

163
00:07:24,387 --> 00:07:27,160
groups treat seeds differently. Rodents chew and

164
00:07:27,160 --> 00:07:28,745
digest seeds, destroying them.

165
00:07:28,905 --> 00:07:31,019
Birds tend to pass seeds intact and

166
00:07:31,019 --> 00:07:33,133
drop them far from the parent plant,

167
00:07:33,133 --> 00:07:35,247
which is exactly what a plant needs.

168
00:07:35,797 --> 00:07:38,442
The receptor explains the split. Later work

169
00:07:38,442 --> 00:07:41,087
from the Julius laboratory compared the bird

170
00:07:41,087 --> 00:07:43,733
version of TRPV1 with the mammalian one.

171
00:07:43,733 --> 00:07:46,378
The bird channel still responds to heat,

172
00:07:46,378 --> 00:07:48,645
but it barely responds to capsaicin.

173
00:07:48,805 --> 00:07:51,242
For birds the molecule is largely silent,

174
00:07:51,242 --> 00:07:53,678
not a deterrent. So capsaicin works as

175
00:07:53,678 --> 00:07:56,115
a filter: it deters the animals that

176
00:07:56,115 --> 00:07:58,551
would destroy the seeds and stays quiet

177
00:07:58,551 --> 00:08:00,640
for the animals that spread them.

178
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:03,992
The same molecule, meeting two versions of

179
00:08:03,992 --> 00:08:07,184
one channel, produces two completely different experiences.

180
00:08:07,734 --> 00:08:10,480
Capsaicin may have more than one job.

181
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:13,227
Field work on wild chilies has also

182
00:08:13,227 --> 00:08:15,973
suggested that it slows fungi that attack

183
00:08:15,973 --> 00:08:18,719
seeds, and that wild plants in wetter,

184
00:08:18,719 --> 00:08:21,073
fungus-heavy places tend to be hotter.

185
00:08:21,233 --> 00:08:24,322
That second role is less settled than the

186
00:08:24,322 --> 00:08:27,411
first. The firm part is narrow: it deters

187
00:08:27,411 --> 00:08:30,500
mammals. It may also slow fungi. Both are

188
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:33,588
inferences about evolutionary pressure, and no experiment can

189
00:08:33,588 --> 00:08:35,905
rerun the history that produced them.

190
00:08:36,455 --> 00:08:39,978
Long before anyone knew the receptor existed, people

191
00:08:39,978 --> 00:08:43,501
wanted to rank chilies. In 1912 a pharmacist

192
00:08:43,501 --> 00:08:47,024
named Wilbur Scoville published a method that used

193
00:08:47,024 --> 00:08:49,666
the only detector available, human tongues.

194
00:08:49,826 --> 00:08:52,539
An extract of dried pepper was diluted in

195
00:08:52,539 --> 00:08:55,253
sugar water, step by step, until a panel

196
00:08:55,253 --> 00:08:57,966
of tasters could no longer detect any heat.

197
00:08:57,966 --> 00:09:00,679
The amount of dilution needed became the score,

198
00:09:00,679 --> 00:09:02,375
now called Scoville heat units.

199
00:09:02,535 --> 00:09:05,955
A pepper that needed more dilution was hotter.

200
00:09:05,955 --> 00:09:09,375
It was clever, and it was entirely subjective.

201
00:09:09,375 --> 00:09:12,795
Not until the TRPV1 work of 1997 did

202
00:09:12,795 --> 00:09:16,215
anyone know what those tasters were actually detecting.

203
00:09:16,765 --> 00:09:19,978
The weakness is built in. Tongues tire, people

204
00:09:19,978 --> 00:09:23,191
differ, and a panel's threshold drifts from day

205
00:09:23,191 --> 00:09:26,404
to day. So laboratories now measure capsaicinoids directly

206
00:09:26,404 --> 00:09:29,618
with high-performance liquid chromatography, which separates the compounds

207
00:09:29,618 --> 00:09:32,429
in an extract and quantifies each one.

208
00:09:32,589 --> 00:09:35,625
The result is a concentration, a chemical fact

209
00:09:35,625 --> 00:09:38,661
rather than a sensation. That concentration is then

210
00:09:38,661 --> 00:09:41,697
converted back into Scoville heat units, so the

211
00:09:41,697 --> 00:09:44,733
familiar scale survives even though the sugar water

212
00:09:44,733 --> 00:09:47,389
and the panel of tasters are gone.

213
00:09:47,939 --> 00:09:51,045
On that converted scale, pure capsaicin sits at

214
00:09:51,045 --> 00:09:54,152
the very top, in the millions, and the

215
00:09:54,152 --> 00:09:57,258
exact figure quoted for it depends on the

216
00:09:57,258 --> 00:10:00,365
conversion used. Treat any such number as a

217
00:10:00,365 --> 00:10:03,083
convention rather than a measurement of experience.

218
00:10:03,243 --> 00:10:05,889
Scoville heat units now report a concentration of

219
00:10:05,889 --> 00:10:08,536
a molecule. They do not measure how much

220
00:10:08,536 --> 00:10:11,182
a given person feels, which depends on their

221
00:10:11,182 --> 00:10:13,829
receptors, their history of exposure and the food

222
00:10:13,829 --> 00:10:15,483
the chili is cooked into.

223
00:10:16,033 --> 00:10:18,694
Here is what this evidence does not establish.

224
00:10:18,694 --> 00:10:21,356
The receptor work settles the mechanism of the

225
00:10:21,356 --> 00:10:24,017
burn and the heat threshold behind it; it

226
00:10:24,017 --> 00:10:26,679
does not measure how much a given person

227
00:10:26,679 --> 00:10:29,340
feels, which varies and is shaped by exposure.

228
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:32,094
Published Scoville figures for named varieties span

229
00:10:32,094 --> 00:10:34,688
wide ranges, because a plant's heat changes

230
00:10:34,688 --> 00:10:37,282
with growing conditions, so any single number

231
00:10:37,282 --> 00:10:39,876
for a variety is a midpoint, not

232
00:10:39,876 --> 00:10:41,729
a fact about every fruit.

233
00:10:41,889 --> 00:10:44,855
The 43 degree threshold is an approximate value

234
00:10:44,855 --> 00:10:47,822
from cells in a dish, and the channel's

235
00:10:47,822 --> 00:10:50,788
sensitivity shifts with its chemical surroundings. And the

236
00:10:50,788 --> 00:10:53,754
account of why chilies evolved capsaicin is the

237
00:10:53,754 --> 00:10:56,350
best supported inference, not a recorded event.

238
00:10:56,900 --> 00:10:59,610
A chili is a message written in the

239
00:10:59,610 --> 00:11:02,321
wrong language on purpose. The molecule speaks to

240
00:11:02,321 --> 00:11:05,031
a channel built to detect heat, and the

241
00:11:05,031 --> 00:11:07,742
nerve, with only one way to answer, says

242
00:11:07,742 --> 00:11:09,097
burn. Nothing is warming.

243
00:11:09,257 --> 00:11:11,585
You are hearing a plant's alarm, set

244
00:11:11,585 --> 00:11:13,914
for mammals, ignored by birds, and read

245
00:11:13,914 --> 00:11:15,577
by your brain as fire.

246
00:11:16,133 --> 00:11:17,856
Why does coffee taste bitter?

247
00:11:18,406 --> 00:11:20,126
Take the caffeine out of a cup,

248
00:11:20,126 --> 00:11:21,601
and most of the bitterness stays.

249
00:11:21,761 --> 00:11:24,992
Coffee tastes bitter mainly because roasting breaks the

250
00:11:24,992 --> 00:11:28,223
bean's chlorogenic acids into new bitter molecules, which

251
00:11:28,223 --> 00:11:31,453
bind bitter taste receptors on the tongue; caffeine

252
00:11:31,453 --> 00:11:34,684
contributes, but only part of the effect. Those

253
00:11:34,684 --> 00:11:37,915
receptors are a family, not a single sensor.

254
00:11:38,075 --> 00:11:40,651
Research supported by the NIH puts the

255
00:11:40,651 --> 00:11:43,227
human count at about 25 types of

256
00:11:43,227 --> 00:11:45,803
bitter receptor. That number tells you the

257
00:11:45,803 --> 00:11:47,275
tongue has many locks.

258
00:11:47,435 --> 00:11:49,395
It does not tell you which

259
00:11:49,395 --> 00:11:51,354
keys a roasted bean is carrying,

260
00:11:51,354 --> 00:11:52,987
or how anyone found out.

261
00:11:53,147 --> 00:11:55,769
That second part is the story, and it

262
00:11:55,769 --> 00:11:58,392
starts with an awkward fact: decaf still bites,

263
00:11:58,392 --> 00:12:01,014
so something else must be doing the work.

264
00:12:01,564 --> 00:12:04,215
Start with the cheapest experiment in food

265
00:12:04,215 --> 00:12:06,865
science: a cup of decaffeinated coffee. Decaffeination

266
00:12:06,865 --> 00:12:09,516
removes most of the caffeine from green

267
00:12:09,516 --> 00:12:12,167
beans before they are roasted, and the

268
00:12:12,167 --> 00:12:14,060
result is still unmistakably bitter.

269
00:12:14,220 --> 00:12:16,907
If caffeine were the main source, decaf

270
00:12:16,907 --> 00:12:19,594
should taste noticeably flat. It does not.

271
00:12:19,594 --> 00:12:22,281
Caffeine is genuinely bitter on its own.

272
00:12:22,281 --> 00:12:24,968
Dissolve the pure compound in water and

273
00:12:24,968 --> 00:12:27,271
you get a clean, sharp bitterness.

274
00:12:27,431 --> 00:12:29,742
So caffeine is a contributor, but it

275
00:12:29,742 --> 00:12:32,052
is not the main source. Coffee stays

276
00:12:32,052 --> 00:12:34,363
bitter without caffeine, because its bitterness was

277
00:12:34,363 --> 00:12:36,343
never mostly caffeine to begin with.

278
00:12:36,893 --> 00:12:39,201
Stability is the second clue. Caffeine is

279
00:12:39,201 --> 00:12:41,509
a bitter alkaloid in the raw bean,

280
00:12:41,509 --> 00:12:43,817
and it is fairly heat-stable, so its

281
00:12:43,817 --> 00:12:46,125
bitterness does not change much with roasting.

282
00:12:46,285 --> 00:12:49,637
PubChem, the chemical database run by the

283
00:12:49,637 --> 00:12:52,990
National Institutes of Health, records its structure:

284
00:12:52,990 --> 00:12:56,342
a small, ring-shaped molecule built from carbon,

285
00:12:56,342 --> 00:12:58,258
hydrogen, nitrogen and oxygen.

286
00:12:58,418 --> 00:13:00,976
Yet anyone who has compared a pale

287
00:13:00,976 --> 00:13:03,534
roast with a very dark one knows

288
00:13:03,534 --> 00:13:06,092
the bitterness changes a great deal. A

289
00:13:06,092 --> 00:13:08,650
molecule whose amount barely moves cannot explain

290
00:13:08,650 --> 00:13:10,842
a taste that moves that much.

291
00:13:11,002 --> 00:13:13,182
Whatever drives the difference has to be

292
00:13:13,182 --> 00:13:15,050
made, or destroyed, in the roaster.

293
00:13:15,600 --> 00:13:18,746
A green coffee bean, taken from the fruit

294
00:13:18,746 --> 00:13:21,891
and dried, tastes grassy and astringent rather than

295
00:13:21,891 --> 00:13:25,037
like coffee. Among its most abundant soluble compounds

296
00:13:25,037 --> 00:13:28,182
is a group called chlorogenic acids. Despite the

297
00:13:28,182 --> 00:13:31,328
name, they have nothing to do with chlorine.

298
00:13:31,488 --> 00:13:34,528
Each one is an ester, a joining of

299
00:13:34,528 --> 00:13:37,569
caffeic acid, or a close relative, with another

300
00:13:37,569 --> 00:13:40,609
molecule called quinic acid. PubChem holds entries for

301
00:13:40,609 --> 00:13:43,269
members of the family, with their structures.

302
00:13:43,429 --> 00:13:45,554
In the raw bean they are more

303
00:13:45,554 --> 00:13:47,678
astringent than bitter. They are better thought

304
00:13:47,678 --> 00:13:49,803
of as raw material, waiting for heat.

305
00:13:50,353 --> 00:13:53,298
Then comes the heat. The American Chemical Society,

306
00:13:53,298 --> 00:13:56,244
describing the chemistry of a roast, puts the

307
00:13:56,244 --> 00:13:59,189
beans above 200 degrees Celsius. At those temperatures

308
00:13:59,189 --> 00:14:02,134
the bean behaves like a small pressure vessel.

309
00:14:02,294 --> 00:14:04,886
Water flashes to steam, sugars and amino

310
00:14:04,886 --> 00:14:07,478
acids run through Maillard reactions that make

311
00:14:07,478 --> 00:14:10,070
brown pigments and aroma, and the chlorogenic

312
00:14:10,070 --> 00:14:12,662
acids start to come apart. Some of

313
00:14:12,662 --> 00:14:14,513
their mass simply breaks down.

314
00:14:14,673 --> 00:14:17,052
Some of it is rebuilt into molecules

315
00:14:17,052 --> 00:14:19,431
that were never in the raw bean.

316
00:14:19,431 --> 00:14:21,811
Roasting is not cooking the bitterness out.

317
00:14:21,811 --> 00:14:23,510
It is cooking it in.

318
00:14:24,060 --> 00:14:26,400
The path from acid to bitterness runs

319
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:28,740
in stages. The first is a loss

320
00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:30,412
of water inside the molecule.

321
00:14:30,572 --> 00:14:33,798
When a chlorogenic acid loses water, its quinic

322
00:14:33,798 --> 00:14:37,024
acid portion closes into a tighter ring, and

323
00:14:37,024 --> 00:14:39,847
the products are called chlorogenic acid lactones.

324
00:14:40,007 --> 00:14:43,162
Lactones taste distinctly bitter, and research reported

325
00:14:43,162 --> 00:14:46,317
by the American Chemical Society identified them

326
00:14:46,317 --> 00:14:49,472
as major bitter compounds in light to

327
00:14:49,472 --> 00:14:52,627
medium roasts. Push the roast further, and

328
00:14:52,627 --> 00:14:54,881
the lactones themselves break down.

329
00:14:55,041 --> 00:14:58,131
Their caffeic acid portions are freed, fragment,

330
00:14:58,131 --> 00:15:01,222
and link together into compounds called phenylindanes.

331
00:15:01,222 --> 00:15:04,312
Those carry a harsher, more lingering bitterness,

332
00:15:04,312 --> 00:15:06,961
and they accumulate in dark roasts.

333
00:15:07,121 --> 00:15:09,815
So the bitterness is harsher with darker roasts,

334
00:15:09,815 --> 00:15:12,172
and it carries a different chemical signature.

335
00:15:12,722 --> 00:15:15,143
Notice what this explains at once. It

336
00:15:15,143 --> 00:15:17,563
explains why darker roasts taste more bitter,

337
00:15:17,563 --> 00:15:19,984
since the pathway keeps generating harsher products

338
00:15:19,984 --> 00:15:21,367
as the heat continues.

339
00:15:21,527 --> 00:15:24,552
It explains why decaf stays bitter, because decaffeination

340
00:15:24,552 --> 00:15:27,577
targets caffeine and leaves the chlorogenic acids behind

341
00:15:27,577 --> 00:15:30,602
to be transformed in the roaster. What it

342
00:15:30,602 --> 00:15:33,627
does not hand you is the exact share

343
00:15:33,627 --> 00:15:36,274
of each compound in a given cup.

344
00:15:36,434 --> 00:15:38,989
That depends on the bean, the roast,

345
00:15:38,989 --> 00:15:41,545
and the brew, and published figures vary

346
00:15:41,545 --> 00:15:43,735
from one study to the next.

347
00:15:44,285 --> 00:15:47,633
How do chemists know which of the many

348
00:15:47,633 --> 00:15:50,981
molecules in a cup is bitter? A brewed

349
00:15:50,981 --> 00:15:54,330
coffee contains hundreds of compounds, and an instrument

350
00:15:54,330 --> 00:15:57,678
alone cannot taste anything. The method used is

351
00:15:57,678 --> 00:15:59,352
often called sensory-guided analysis.

352
00:15:59,512 --> 00:16:02,847
First, liquid chromatography separates the brew into fractions,

353
00:16:02,847 --> 00:16:06,181
each holding a narrower set of molecules. Then

354
00:16:06,181 --> 00:16:09,516
trained tasting panels sample each fraction, diluting it

355
00:16:09,516 --> 00:16:12,434
step by step until the bitterness disappears.

356
00:16:12,594 --> 00:16:15,029
A fraction that stays bitter through

357
00:16:15,029 --> 00:16:17,058
many dilutions holds something potent.

358
00:16:17,218 --> 00:16:20,516
Only then do the instruments take over, and

359
00:16:20,516 --> 00:16:23,814
mass spectrometry weighs the molecules and their fragments,

360
00:16:23,814 --> 00:16:27,112
while nuclear magnetic resonance maps how their atoms

361
00:16:27,112 --> 00:16:29,586
connect, until the structure is confirmed.

362
00:16:30,136 --> 00:16:33,399
The next step closes the loop. Once a

363
00:16:33,399 --> 00:16:36,662
structure is proposed, chemists make the compound from

364
00:16:36,662 --> 00:16:39,925
scratch, purify it, and taste the synthetic version.

365
00:16:39,925 --> 00:16:43,188
If it reproduces the bitterness at the expected

366
00:16:43,188 --> 00:16:44,819
strength, the identification holds.

367
00:16:44,979 --> 00:16:47,685
A key measure here is the dilution

368
00:16:47,685 --> 00:16:50,390
threshold, the weakest concentration at which a

369
00:16:50,390 --> 00:16:52,323
trained panel still detects bitterness.

370
00:16:52,483 --> 00:16:54,785
Comparing that threshold with how much of the

371
00:16:54,785 --> 00:16:57,087
compound is actually in a cup tells you

372
00:16:57,087 --> 00:16:59,101
whether it matters, or is merely present.

373
00:16:59,261 --> 00:17:01,677
This kind of work fills the pages

374
00:17:01,677 --> 00:17:04,093
of the Journal of Agricultural and Food

375
00:17:04,093 --> 00:17:06,509
Chemistry, published by the American Chemical Society.

376
00:17:06,669 --> 00:17:09,204
The threshold is not a law of nature:

377
00:17:09,204 --> 00:17:11,739
it varies between tasters, which is why panels

378
00:17:11,739 --> 00:17:13,640
are trained and results are averaged.

379
00:17:14,190 --> 00:17:16,960
There is a stricter test still, and it

380
00:17:16,960 --> 00:17:19,729
is the closest thing this field has to

381
00:17:19,729 --> 00:17:22,153
a controlled experiment. Researchers rebuild the cup.

382
00:17:22,313 --> 00:17:24,895
They take each identified compound at the concentration

383
00:17:24,895 --> 00:17:27,478
measured in a real brew, dissolve the set

384
00:17:27,478 --> 00:17:30,061
in water, and ask a panel to compare

385
00:17:30,061 --> 00:17:32,643
the mixture with the coffee it came from.

386
00:17:32,803 --> 00:17:35,509
If the rebuilt version tastes about as bitter,

387
00:17:35,509 --> 00:17:38,215
the list is reasonably complete. Then they leave

388
00:17:38,215 --> 00:17:40,921
compounds out, one group at a time. When

389
00:17:40,921 --> 00:17:43,627
removing a group makes the mixture noticeably less

390
00:17:43,627 --> 00:17:45,995
bitter, that group is pulling its weight.

391
00:17:46,155 --> 00:17:48,872
Omission tests of this kind are how caffeine's

392
00:17:48,872 --> 00:17:51,249
modest role was measured, rather than assumed.

393
00:17:51,799 --> 00:17:54,541
Molecules are only half of the story.

394
00:17:54,541 --> 00:17:57,282
Bitterness is something a cell decides. On

395
00:17:57,282 --> 00:18:00,024
the tongue, and on the soft palate,

396
00:18:00,024 --> 00:18:02,766
taste buds hold receptor cells, and some

397
00:18:02,766 --> 00:18:05,116
of those cells carry bitter receptors.

398
00:18:05,276 --> 00:18:07,873
These are proteins that span the cell

399
00:18:07,873 --> 00:18:10,470
membrane. When the right molecule docks on

400
00:18:10,470 --> 00:18:13,067
the outside, the receptor changes shape and

401
00:18:13,067 --> 00:18:15,664
triggers a signal inside the cell, and

402
00:18:15,664 --> 00:18:17,890
a nerve carries the message on.

403
00:18:18,050 --> 00:18:21,083
As research supported by the NIH has

404
00:18:21,083 --> 00:18:24,116
described, humans carry about 25 types of

405
00:18:24,116 --> 00:18:27,149
bitter receptor, and many of them respond

406
00:18:27,149 --> 00:18:28,882
to several different molecules.

407
00:18:29,432 --> 00:18:32,127
The way scientists learned which molecule triggers which

408
00:18:32,127 --> 00:18:34,822
receptor is not by tasting. The method is

409
00:18:34,822 --> 00:18:37,517
a receptor assay. They take a single receptor

410
00:18:37,517 --> 00:18:40,212
gene and insert it into cultured cells that

411
00:18:40,212 --> 00:18:41,896
do not normally taste anything.

412
00:18:42,056 --> 00:18:44,963
They load those cells with a dye that

413
00:18:44,963 --> 00:18:47,870
glows when calcium floods in, which is what

414
00:18:47,870 --> 00:18:50,777
happens when the receptor fires, producing a calcium

415
00:18:50,777 --> 00:18:53,683
signal. Then they add a test compound. If

416
00:18:53,683 --> 00:18:56,227
the cells light up, that receptor responds.

417
00:18:56,387 --> 00:18:59,268
Repeating this across the family maps which

418
00:18:59,268 --> 00:19:02,150
molecule activates which receptor. Caffeine, tested this

419
00:19:02,150 --> 00:19:05,031
way, activates several of them. Roast compounds

420
00:19:05,031 --> 00:19:07,913
can be screened the same way, though

421
00:19:07,913 --> 00:19:09,971
fewer have been fully mapped.

422
00:19:10,521 --> 00:19:13,027
This is why bitterness is a response in

423
00:19:13,027 --> 00:19:15,533
the taster, not a fixed property of the

424
00:19:15,533 --> 00:19:17,726
molecule. The receptor genes vary between people.

425
00:19:17,886 --> 00:19:20,782
Some versions of a receptor bind a given

426
00:19:20,782 --> 00:19:23,677
compound strongly, others weakly, and some people barely

427
00:19:23,677 --> 00:19:25,849
detect certain bitter molecules at all.

428
00:19:26,009 --> 00:19:28,706
The same cup can register as harsh to

429
00:19:28,706 --> 00:19:31,403
one person and mild to another, without either

430
00:19:31,403 --> 00:19:34,101
of them being wrong. The chemistry in the

431
00:19:34,101 --> 00:19:36,798
cup is the same. The sensors are not.

432
00:19:37,348 --> 00:19:40,396
The roaster makes the bitter molecules. The brew

433
00:19:40,396 --> 00:19:43,444
decides how many of them leave the grounds.

434
00:19:43,444 --> 00:19:46,491
Hot water is a solvent, and different compounds

435
00:19:46,491 --> 00:19:49,539
dissolve at different rates. Bright acids and many

436
00:19:49,539 --> 00:19:51,444
aroma compounds come out early.

437
00:19:51,604 --> 00:19:54,458
Some of the heavier, more bitter roast products

438
00:19:54,458 --> 00:19:57,312
come out more slowly. So anything that pushes

439
00:19:57,312 --> 00:20:00,166
extraction further, whether a finer grind, longer contact

440
00:20:00,166 --> 00:20:03,020
time or hotter water, tends to pull more

441
00:20:03,020 --> 00:20:04,804
bitter compounds into the cup.

442
00:20:04,964 --> 00:20:08,835
Coffee professionals call the extreme over-extraction, and its

443
00:20:08,835 --> 00:20:12,223
usual signature is a dry, lingering bitterness.

444
00:20:12,773 --> 00:20:15,842
There is also what goes in afterwards. Sugar

445
00:20:15,842 --> 00:20:18,910
does not remove a single bitter molecule from

446
00:20:18,910 --> 00:20:21,979
the cup. It lowers how bitter the cup

447
00:20:21,979 --> 00:20:25,047
seems, because sweet and bitter signals interact as

448
00:20:25,047 --> 00:20:26,965
the brain combines taste information.

449
00:20:27,125 --> 00:20:29,734
Milk is a different case. Proteins in milk

450
00:20:29,734 --> 00:20:32,342
can bind some of coffee's polyphenols, and fat

451
00:20:32,342 --> 00:20:34,951
changes how compounds spread across the tongue, though

452
00:20:34,951 --> 00:20:37,560
how much each effect matters in a real

453
00:20:37,560 --> 00:20:38,864
cup is still debated.

454
00:20:39,024 --> 00:20:41,650
In both cases the bitterness is changed

455
00:20:41,650 --> 00:20:44,277
in perception, not deleted from the chemistry.

456
00:20:44,827 --> 00:20:47,831
This evidence does not establish a fixed recipe

457
00:20:47,831 --> 00:20:50,836
for bitterness. Most studies work on particular beans,

458
00:20:50,836 --> 00:20:53,840
roasted in particular ways, brewed by particular methods,

459
00:20:53,840 --> 00:20:56,845
and the proportions they report do not transfer

460
00:20:56,845 --> 00:20:58,347
cleanly to every cup.

461
00:20:58,507 --> 00:21:00,347
The contribution of other roast

462
00:21:00,347 --> 00:21:01,819
products is less settled.

463
00:21:01,979 --> 00:21:05,055
Melanoidins, the large brown polymers built during roasting,

464
00:21:05,055 --> 00:21:08,131
are proposed as contributors, but the share from

465
00:21:08,131 --> 00:21:11,207
melanoidins is hard to pin down because they

466
00:21:11,207 --> 00:21:14,283
are too large and varied to isolate cleanly.

467
00:21:14,443 --> 00:21:16,944
Receptor maps come from cells in dishes,

468
00:21:16,944 --> 00:21:19,445
not from a living mouth, where saliva,

469
00:21:19,445 --> 00:21:21,232
aroma and temperature all intervene.

470
00:21:21,392 --> 00:21:24,260
What is firm is narrower: roasting creates bitter

471
00:21:24,260 --> 00:21:27,129
compounds that the raw bean lacks, and caffeine

472
00:21:27,129 --> 00:21:29,280
alone cannot account for the taste.

473
00:21:29,830 --> 00:21:32,468
A green bean holds the raw material. Heat

474
00:21:32,468 --> 00:21:35,105
rebuilds it into molecules the plant never made,

475
00:21:35,105 --> 00:21:37,743
water carries some of them out, and a

476
00:21:37,743 --> 00:21:40,381
family of receptors renders its verdict. The bitterness

477
00:21:40,381 --> 00:21:42,689
in the cup is not one substance.

478
00:21:42,849 --> 00:21:44,742
It is a record of the roast.

479
00:21:45,300 --> 00:21:47,097
Why does honey never spoil?

480
00:21:47,647 --> 00:21:50,252
Add a little water to honey, and yeast

481
00:21:50,252 --> 00:21:52,857
can start eating it. That is the clue.

482
00:21:52,857 --> 00:21:55,462
Honey resists spoiling because it holds so little

483
00:21:55,462 --> 00:21:58,067
water, and so much sugar, that microbes cannot

484
00:21:58,067 --> 00:21:59,695
draw enough water to grow.

485
00:21:59,855 --> 00:22:02,889
On screen is the figure that sets the

486
00:22:02,889 --> 00:22:05,923
line in practice. USDA grade standards for extracted

487
00:22:05,923 --> 00:22:08,957
honey cap the moisture of the top grades

488
00:22:08,957 --> 00:22:11,991
at 18.6 percent. That number does not describe

489
00:22:11,991 --> 00:22:13,887
a preservative that someone added.

490
00:22:14,047 --> 00:22:16,819
It describes something that is missing. And

491
00:22:16,819 --> 00:22:19,591
it overturns a familiar assumption. Honey is

492
00:22:19,591 --> 00:22:22,363
not immortal by nature. It stays protected

493
00:22:22,363 --> 00:22:25,135
only as long as it stays dry.

494
00:22:25,685 --> 00:22:28,566
To a bacterium, a jar of honey looks

495
00:22:28,566 --> 00:22:31,447
like an enormous meal. It is almost entirely

496
00:22:31,447 --> 00:22:34,327
sugar, the fuel most microbes prefer. The problem

497
00:22:34,327 --> 00:22:36,848
is not food. The problem is water.

498
00:22:37,008 --> 00:22:40,441
Food scientists measure that with a quantity called

499
00:22:40,441 --> 00:22:43,445
water activity, the term now on screen.

500
00:22:43,605 --> 00:22:46,171
Food chemists, in research published by the American

501
00:22:46,171 --> 00:22:48,736
Chemical Society, treat it as the water in

502
00:22:48,736 --> 00:22:51,302
a food that is free to move and

503
00:22:51,302 --> 00:22:53,868
take part in reactions, rather than the total

504
00:22:53,868 --> 00:22:55,792
amount present. The scale is simple.

505
00:22:55,952 --> 00:22:58,545
Pure water sits at 1, and a

506
00:22:58,545 --> 00:23:01,138
completely dry material sits near 0. Every

507
00:23:01,138 --> 00:23:03,731
microbe has a floor on that scale

508
00:23:03,731 --> 00:23:06,324
below which it cannot grow. Most bacteria

509
00:23:06,324 --> 00:23:08,917
need conditions close to the wet end.

510
00:23:09,077 --> 00:23:12,254
Some molds and specialised yeasts can manage drier

511
00:23:12,254 --> 00:23:15,034
ground, but each still has a limit.

512
00:23:15,584 --> 00:23:18,564
Here is the distinction that matters, and the

513
00:23:18,564 --> 00:23:21,544
screen splits it in two. Water content is

514
00:23:21,544 --> 00:23:24,524
how much water is present. Water activity is

515
00:23:24,524 --> 00:23:27,504
how much of that water is actually available.

516
00:23:27,664 --> 00:23:29,811
Honey contains a fair amount of water

517
00:23:29,811 --> 00:23:31,959
by weight. But nearly all of it

518
00:23:31,959 --> 00:23:34,106
is held tightly by sugar molecules, which

519
00:23:34,106 --> 00:23:35,947
bind to water through hydrogen bonds.

520
00:23:36,107 --> 00:23:38,424
A water molecule clinging to a sugar is

521
00:23:38,424 --> 00:23:40,740
not free to cross into a cell. So

522
00:23:40,740 --> 00:23:43,057
a food can be wet to the touch

523
00:23:43,057 --> 00:23:45,373
and still be a desert to a microbe.

524
00:23:45,373 --> 00:23:47,400
Honey is exactly that kind of desert.

525
00:23:47,560 --> 00:23:49,877
Its water activity sits well below the

526
00:23:49,877 --> 00:23:52,193
line most bacteria need, although the published

527
00:23:52,193 --> 00:23:54,510
values vary from sample to sample and

528
00:23:54,510 --> 00:23:56,496
from one floral source to another.

529
00:23:57,046 --> 00:23:59,782
How is that known, rather than assumed? On

530
00:23:59,782 --> 00:24:02,518
screen is the instrument chain. A small sample

531
00:24:02,518 --> 00:24:05,255
of honey goes into a sealed chamber. Water

532
00:24:05,255 --> 00:24:07,991
escapes from the sample into the air above

533
00:24:07,991 --> 00:24:10,043
it until the two reach balance.

534
00:24:10,203 --> 00:24:12,484
The instrument then measures the humidity of

535
00:24:12,484 --> 00:24:14,765
that trapped air, commonly by cooling a

536
00:24:14,765 --> 00:24:17,046
tiny mirror until dew forms on it.

537
00:24:17,206 --> 00:24:19,789
The temperature of that dew point tells you

538
00:24:19,789 --> 00:24:22,372
how much water vapour is present, and from

539
00:24:22,372 --> 00:24:24,955
that the device reports water activity. Total moisture,

540
00:24:24,955 --> 00:24:27,538
the figure in the grade standards, is usually

541
00:24:27,538 --> 00:24:28,830
measured a different way.

542
00:24:28,990 --> 00:24:32,348
A refractometer reads how strongly the honey bends

543
00:24:32,348 --> 00:24:35,706
light, and because sugar and water bend light

544
00:24:35,706 --> 00:24:39,064
differently, that reading converts to moisture. Two instruments,

545
00:24:39,064 --> 00:24:41,582
two different questions, one consistent picture.

546
00:24:42,132 --> 00:24:44,645
That dryness is manufactured, and the screen

547
00:24:44,645 --> 00:24:47,157
walks through the stages. Worker bees collect

548
00:24:47,157 --> 00:24:49,670
nectar, which is far wetter than finished

549
00:24:49,670 --> 00:24:52,182
honey. Back in the hive, they add

550
00:24:52,182 --> 00:24:53,977
enzymes from their own glands.

551
00:24:54,137 --> 00:24:57,527
One of them, invertase, splits sucrose into its

552
00:24:57,527 --> 00:25:00,918
two simpler sugars, glucose and fructose. Then the

553
00:25:00,918 --> 00:25:04,308
drying begins. Bees spread the liquid thinly through

554
00:25:04,308 --> 00:25:07,698
the comb and fan their wings, driving a

555
00:25:07,698 --> 00:25:10,665
current of warm air across the cells.

556
00:25:10,825 --> 00:25:13,442
Water evaporates. Only when the honey has

557
00:25:13,442 --> 00:25:16,060
thickened enough do the bees cap each

558
00:25:16,060 --> 00:25:18,677
cell with wax. The moisture falls before

559
00:25:18,677 --> 00:25:20,921
the seal goes on, not after.

560
00:25:21,471 --> 00:25:24,374
Beekeepers read that sign. A frame with most

561
00:25:24,374 --> 00:25:27,277
of its cells capped is usually ripe enough

562
00:25:27,277 --> 00:25:30,180
to harvest, and many beekeepers confirm it with

563
00:25:30,180 --> 00:25:31,994
a handheld refractometer before extracting.

564
00:25:32,154 --> 00:25:34,708
Honey taken too early, from open cells, can

565
00:25:34,708 --> 00:25:37,263
carry too much water. That is exactly the

566
00:25:37,263 --> 00:25:39,498
honey most likely to go wrong later.

567
00:25:39,658 --> 00:25:41,956
So the stability you find in a

568
00:25:41,956 --> 00:25:44,253
jar is partly the work of the

569
00:25:44,253 --> 00:25:46,551
colony and partly the judgement of whoever

570
00:25:46,551 --> 00:25:48,849
harvested it. Nothing about the sugar alone

571
00:25:48,849 --> 00:25:50,490
guarantees it. The process does.

572
00:25:51,040 --> 00:25:54,077
Now follow a single microbe into the

573
00:25:54,077 --> 00:25:57,114
jar. The glowing cross-section shows a bacterial

574
00:25:57,114 --> 00:26:00,150
cell with a thin membrane around it.

575
00:26:00,150 --> 00:26:03,187
Inside, the cell's contents are watery. Outside,

576
00:26:03,187 --> 00:26:06,224
the honey is a concentrated sugar solution.

577
00:26:06,384 --> 00:26:09,063
Water moves across a membrane from the side

578
00:26:09,063 --> 00:26:11,742
where it is more available toward the side

579
00:26:11,742 --> 00:26:14,421
where it is less available. This is osmosis.

580
00:26:14,581 --> 00:26:17,614
So instead of the cell drawing water in,

581
00:26:17,614 --> 00:26:20,647
water is drawn outward through the membrane, into

582
00:26:20,647 --> 00:26:23,680
the honey. The cell shrinks. Its internal machinery,

583
00:26:23,680 --> 00:26:26,712
which depends on water to function, slows and

584
00:26:26,712 --> 00:26:28,987
stops. Many cells simply cannot recover.

585
00:26:29,537 --> 00:26:32,304
It is worth being exact about what is

586
00:26:32,304 --> 00:26:35,071
doing the work here, and the screen holds

587
00:26:35,071 --> 00:26:37,838
both sides. Osmotic stress is water leaving the

588
00:26:37,838 --> 00:26:39,913
cell. It is not a poison.

589
00:26:40,073 --> 00:26:43,358
Glucose and fructose, whose structures are catalogued by

590
00:26:43,358 --> 00:26:46,643
PubChem, are ordinary sugars that cells use for

591
00:26:46,643 --> 00:26:49,929
energy every day. Diluted, they would feed the

592
00:26:49,929 --> 00:26:51,982
very organisms they now suppress.

593
00:26:52,142 --> 00:26:54,529
That is why the same substance can

594
00:26:54,529 --> 00:26:56,915
be a preservative at one concentration and

595
00:26:56,915 --> 00:26:59,302
a growth medium at another. The protection

596
00:26:59,302 --> 00:27:01,688
is a property of the mixture, not

597
00:27:01,688 --> 00:27:03,734
of any single molecule in it.

598
00:27:04,284 --> 00:27:06,932
Dryness is the main defence, but it

599
00:27:06,932 --> 00:27:09,580
is not the only one, and the

600
00:27:09,580 --> 00:27:12,227
chain on screen shows the next. Bees

601
00:27:12,227 --> 00:27:14,497
add an enzyme called glucose oxidase.

602
00:27:14,657 --> 00:27:17,397
It acts on glucose and turns it

603
00:27:17,397 --> 00:27:20,138
into gluconic acid, releasing hydrogen peroxide as

604
00:27:20,138 --> 00:27:22,878
a by-product. Gluconic acid is the main

605
00:27:22,878 --> 00:27:25,619
acid in honey, and it is a

606
00:27:25,619 --> 00:27:28,359
large part of why honey is acidic.

607
00:27:28,519 --> 00:27:31,626
Many bacteria grow poorly in acidic conditions.

608
00:27:31,626 --> 00:27:34,733
Hydrogen peroxide, also catalogued by PubChem, is

609
00:27:34,733 --> 00:27:37,840
reactive and damages cells. Together they form

610
00:27:37,840 --> 00:27:40,503
a second barrier behind the first.

611
00:27:41,053 --> 00:27:44,362
There is a subtlety in that chemistry, and

612
00:27:44,362 --> 00:27:47,671
it comes from careful measurement rather than intuition.

613
00:27:47,671 --> 00:27:50,980
Composition surveys from the USDA's Agricultural Research Service,

614
00:27:50,980 --> 00:27:54,288
and laboratory work since, found that glucose oxidase

615
00:27:54,288 --> 00:27:56,770
is fairly quiet in full-strength honey.

616
00:27:56,930 --> 00:27:59,922
It works much more actively once honey is

617
00:27:59,922 --> 00:28:02,914
diluted. That means the peroxide defence is strongest

618
00:28:02,914 --> 00:28:05,907
at precisely the moment the dryness defence weakens.

619
00:28:05,907 --> 00:28:08,899
It is a fallback, not a front line.

620
00:28:08,899 --> 00:28:11,517
The acidity, meanwhile, is real but variable.

621
00:28:11,677 --> 00:28:14,764
Published values differ across floral sources, so

622
00:28:14,764 --> 00:28:17,410
no single number describes every honey.

623
00:28:17,960 --> 00:28:20,466
The checklist on screen sorts what honey

624
00:28:20,466 --> 00:28:22,972
has from what it lacks. It has

625
00:28:22,972 --> 00:28:25,477
low water. It has concentrated sugar. It

626
00:28:25,477 --> 00:28:27,983
has acidity. What it does not have

627
00:28:27,983 --> 00:28:29,773
is protection against absorbed moisture.

628
00:28:29,933 --> 00:28:32,153
Honey is hygroscopic, meaning it pulls water

629
00:28:32,153 --> 00:28:34,373
vapour out of the air. Leave a

630
00:28:34,373 --> 00:28:36,593
jar open in a humid kitchen and

631
00:28:36,593 --> 00:28:38,813
the surface layer slowly takes on water.

632
00:28:38,973 --> 00:28:41,618
The sugar near the top is diluted,

633
00:28:41,618 --> 00:28:43,885
and the water activity there rises.

634
00:28:44,435 --> 00:28:47,205
That is the failure point. Some yeasts are

635
00:28:47,205 --> 00:28:49,975
specialists in sugary, dry environments, and they are

636
00:28:49,975 --> 00:28:52,744
often already present in honey in small numbers,

637
00:28:52,744 --> 00:28:55,514
dormant. Give them a slightly wetter layer and

638
00:28:55,514 --> 00:28:56,899
they can wake up.

639
00:28:57,059 --> 00:28:59,764
They ferment the sugars into alcohol and carbon

640
00:28:59,764 --> 00:29:02,470
dioxide. The honey can foam, smell sour, and

641
00:29:02,470 --> 00:29:05,175
taste of alcohol. This is the same basic

642
00:29:05,175 --> 00:29:07,880
chemistry brewers use to make mead on purpose.

643
00:29:08,040 --> 00:29:10,675
So the honest answer to whether honey

644
00:29:10,675 --> 00:29:13,310
never spoils is that honey spoils readily,

645
00:29:13,310 --> 00:29:15,945
by fermentation, once its water rises. The

646
00:29:15,945 --> 00:29:18,580
claim of permanence is really a claim

647
00:29:18,580 --> 00:29:20,462
about a sealed, dry jar.

648
00:29:21,012 --> 00:29:23,948
Many people throw out honey that is not

649
00:29:23,948 --> 00:29:26,884
spoiled at all. The screen shows why. Over

650
00:29:26,884 --> 00:29:29,819
time, a lot of honey turns cloudy and

651
00:29:29,819 --> 00:29:32,755
grainy. This is crystallization, glucose coming out of

652
00:29:32,755 --> 00:29:34,223
solution as solid crystals.

653
00:29:34,383 --> 00:29:37,242
Glucose dissolves less readily than fructose, so

654
00:29:37,242 --> 00:29:40,101
honeys richer in glucose tend to crystallize

655
00:29:40,101 --> 00:29:42,959
sooner. It is not spoilage. The sugar

656
00:29:42,959 --> 00:29:45,818
is the same sugar, arranged differently. There

657
00:29:45,818 --> 00:29:47,860
is one catch worth knowing.

658
00:29:48,020 --> 00:29:50,354
As glucose locks into crystals, the remaining

659
00:29:50,354 --> 00:29:52,688
liquid holds a little more of the

660
00:29:52,688 --> 00:29:55,022
water, which can raise the fermentation risk

661
00:29:55,022 --> 00:29:56,356
in that liquid portion.

662
00:29:56,906 --> 00:30:00,271
Stored honey also changes chemically, even sealed and

663
00:30:00,271 --> 00:30:03,635
dry. Its colour gradually darkens and its flavour

664
00:30:03,635 --> 00:30:06,159
shifts, especially when it is warmed.

665
00:30:06,319 --> 00:30:09,994
One of the compounds that builds up is

666
00:30:09,994 --> 00:30:13,669
hydroxymethylfurfural, a breakdown product of sugars in acidic

667
00:30:13,669 --> 00:30:17,344
conditions, also catalogued by PubChem. Laboratories track it

668
00:30:17,344 --> 00:30:21,018
with liquid chromatography, which separates the compounds in

669
00:30:21,018 --> 00:30:24,234
a sample so each can be measured.

670
00:30:24,394 --> 00:30:26,704
Rising levels indicate that honey has been

671
00:30:26,704 --> 00:30:29,015
heated or stored for a long time.

672
00:30:29,015 --> 00:30:31,325
That makes it a quality marker, not

673
00:30:31,325 --> 00:30:32,645
proof of microbial decay.

674
00:30:32,805 --> 00:30:34,741
A jar can be old, darker,

675
00:30:34,741 --> 00:30:36,677
and stronger tasting, and still be

676
00:30:36,677 --> 00:30:38,613
free of anything growing in it.

677
00:30:39,163 --> 00:30:41,957
As the sequence plays out, notice what the

678
00:30:41,957 --> 00:30:44,751
visuals never do. No graphic shows honey as

679
00:30:44,751 --> 00:30:47,545
a substance that kills on contact, because that

680
00:30:47,545 --> 00:30:50,340
is not the mechanism. The cross-section shows water

681
00:30:50,340 --> 00:30:52,086
moving, not a toxin acting.

682
00:30:52,246 --> 00:30:55,718
The checklist keeps one line unticked, because the

683
00:30:55,718 --> 00:30:59,190
protection really does have a gap. And the

684
00:30:59,190 --> 00:31:02,662
instruments on screen, the dew point chamber, the

685
00:31:02,662 --> 00:31:06,134
refractometer, the chromatograph, each answer a different question.

686
00:31:06,134 --> 00:31:08,304
Stability is not one measurement.

687
00:31:08,464 --> 00:31:10,390
It is several measurements agreeing.

688
00:31:10,940 --> 00:31:14,151
Here is what this evidence does not establish.

689
00:31:14,151 --> 00:31:17,362
It explains why microbes cannot grow in dry

690
00:31:17,362 --> 00:31:20,172
honey, and how honey ferments when wet.

691
00:31:20,332 --> 00:31:23,781
It does not tell you how long any

692
00:31:23,781 --> 00:31:27,229
particular jar will stay unchanged, because moisture, floral

693
00:31:27,229 --> 00:31:30,247
source, storage temperature and handling all vary.

694
00:31:30,407 --> 00:31:33,214
It also does not settle the popular

695
00:31:33,214 --> 00:31:36,021
stories of edible honey recovered from ancient

696
00:31:36,021 --> 00:31:38,828
tombs, repeated widely, including in a Smithsonian

697
00:31:38,828 --> 00:31:40,833
feature on honey's shelf life.

698
00:31:40,993 --> 00:31:43,782
Those accounts are mostly anecdotal, and the

699
00:31:43,782 --> 00:31:46,572
published record of laboratory analysis on such

700
00:31:46,572 --> 00:31:49,361
samples is thin. And nothing here is

701
00:31:49,361 --> 00:31:52,151
a statement about what is safe to

702
00:31:52,151 --> 00:31:54,940
eat. It describes chemistry in a jar.

703
00:31:55,490 --> 00:31:57,919
So the honey on your shelf is not

704
00:31:57,919 --> 00:32:00,348
immortal. It is an engineered scarcity, a liquid

705
00:32:00,348 --> 00:32:02,776
made so short of free water that life

706
00:32:02,776 --> 00:32:05,205
cannot get a grip on it. Bees build

707
00:32:05,205 --> 00:32:07,634
that condition, and a sealed lid keeps it.

708
00:32:07,794 --> 00:32:11,005
Take the water back, and the oldest preservation

709
00:32:11,005 --> 00:32:13,815
trick in the kitchen quietly undoes itself.

710
00:32:14,367 --> 00:32:16,969
So that is what is inside chili, coffee

711
00:32:16,969 --> 00:32:19,571
and honey: not a secret recipe, but chemistry

712
00:32:19,571 --> 00:32:22,173
you can measure. Every answer in this video

713
00:32:22,173 --> 00:32:24,775
came from someone who refused to stop at

714
00:32:24,775 --> 00:32:26,727
the taste and looked inside instead.

715
00:32:26,887 --> 00:32:29,823
Next time: Why does salt make food

716
00:32:29,823 --> 00:32:32,759
taste better? Stay curious, and stay hungry.
